Hey experts!

This is less a feature and more of a design question. I am trying to understand whether an alternative implementation (below) is feasible or was intentionally avoided. If the latter, please share why, would love to learn!

## Question
Is there a reason we do not use hardware integer atomics to implement atomicMax and atomicMin for floating point? 

Below is an implementation from StackOverflow ([source](https://stackoverflow.com/a/72461459)), allegedly reported [here](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17399119/how-do-i-use-atomicmax-on-floating-point-values-in-cuda/51549250#comment138522534_51549250) to outperform[^1] a CAS loop which CCCL currently uses (see below). Also CUTLASS uses this implementation as shown [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/ebf3165efba92012b4f0eff001bc22d9a4e2af39/include/cutlass/functional.h#L963) and [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cutlass/blob/ebf3165efba92012b4f0eff001bc22d9a4e2af39/examples/41_fused_multi_head_attention/kernel_forward.h#L86).

```cpp
// Source - https://stackoverflow.com/a/72461459
// Posted by timothygiraffe
// Retrieved 2025-12-18, License - CC BY-SA 4.0

__device__ __forceinline__ float atomicMinFloat(float* addr, float value) {
    float old;
    old = !signbit(value) ? __int_as_float(atomicMin((int*)addr, __float_as_int(value))) :
        __uint_as_float(atomicMax((unsigned int*)addr, __float_as_uint(value)));

    return old;
}

__device__ __forceinline__ float atomicMaxFloat(float* addr, float value) {
    float old;
    old = !signbit(value) ? __int_as_float(atomicMax((int*)addr, __float_as_int(value))) :
        __uint_as_float(atomicMin((unsigned int*)addr, __float_as_uint(value)));

    return old;
}
```

## NaN inputs
My understanding is that this is good for finite floats, infinities, and ±0 but does not implement IEEE fminf/fmaxf-style NaN semantics. 

## Current libcu++ call chain for fetch_max<float>
Today, an `atomic_ref<float>...fetch_max(...)` dispatches to a CAS loop via the following path:
- `fetch_max()` calls `__atomic_fetch_max_dispatch` [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/blob/7341abde58bf652e1d3c1aec1b1a07650577e5cd/libcudacxx/include/cuda/__atomic/atomic.h#L104)
- `__atomic_fetch_max_dispatch` calls `__atomic_fetch_max_cuda` [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/blob/7341abde58bf652e1d3c1aec1b1a07650577e5cd/libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/__atomic/types/base.h#L224)
- From [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/blob/7341abde58bf652e1d3c1aec1b1a07650577e5cd/libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/__atomic/functions/cuda_ptx_generated_helper.h#L177), float is not `native_minmax`, so `__atomic_fetch_max_cuda` calls `__atomic_fetch_update_cuda` [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/blob/7341abde58bf652e1d3c1aec1b1a07650577e5cd/libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/__atomic/functions/cuda_ptx_derived.h#L437).
- `__atomic_fetch_update_cuda` is a CAS loop as defined [here](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/blob/7341abde58bf652e1d3c1aec1b1a07650577e5cd/libcudacxx/include/cuda/std/__atomic/functions/cuda_ptx_derived.h#L114).

[^1]: I have not benchmarked this implementation myself, so I cannot personally vouch for the speedup 🙂

