We recently ran into a bug where `cub::DeviceReduce::Min/Max` would produce the wrong result for a user defined type which did not specilize `cuda::std::numeric_limits<T>` (see #5264 and NVBug 5391162). The latter is used internally in CUB to generate the initial value when it is not user provided. This leads to suprising behavior:

```c++
struct S {
    int value;

    friend __host__ __device__ bool operator<(S a, S b) {
        return a.value < b.value;
    }
};

int main() {
    thrust::device_vector<S> in(10, S{42});
    thrust::device_vector<S> out(1, thrust::no_init);

    size_t temp_storage_bytes = 0;
    cub::DeviceReduce::Min(nullptr, temp_storage_bytes, in.begin(), out.begin(), in.size());
    thrust::device_vector<char> temp_storage(temp_storage_bytes, thrust::no_init);
    cub::DeviceReduce::Min(thrust::raw_pointer_cast(temp_storage.data()), temp_storage_bytes, in.begin(), out.begin(), in.size());

    std::cout << "Result " << ((S)out[0]).value; // prints 0
}
```
https://cuda.godbolt.org/z/qzhx4rqEv


Here, the user defined type `S` is comparable, so we can reduce a vector of `S`, each containing the value `42`. The result is an `S` containing `0` though (which is not part of the reduced sequence), because `cuda::std::numeric_limits<S>::max()`, the identity of `Min`, produced a default constructed `S`.

The workaround for the user would be to either provide an inital value, or specialize `cuda::std::numeric_limits<S>`.

I would like to suggest to statically assert that `cuda::std::numeric_limits` must be specialized when no initial value is provided. This is a source breaking change, but it almost certainly uncovers a bug.

